  "statement": "You are given an `n x n` `grid` where we place some `1 x 1 x 1` cubes that are axis-aligned with the `x`, `y`, and `z` axes.\n\nEach value `v = grid[i][j]` represents a tower of `v` cubes placed on top of the cell `(i, j)`.\n\nWe view the projection of these cubes onto the `xy`, `yz`, and `zx` planes.\n\nA **projection** is like a shadow, that maps our **3-dimensional** figure to a **2-dimensional** plane. We are viewing the \"shadow\" when looking at the cubes from the top, the front, and the side.\n\nReturn _the total area of all three projections_.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n![](./assets/projection-area-of-3d-shapes-1.png)\n```\nInput: grid = [[1,2],[3,4]]\nOutput: 17\nExplanation: Here are the three projections (\"shadows\") of the shape made with each axis-aligned plane.\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: grid = [[2]]\nOutput: 5\n```\n\n**Example 3:**\n\n```\nInput: grid = [[1,0],[0,2]]\nOutput: 8\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `n == grid.length == grid[i].length`\n*   `1 <= n <= 50`\n*   `0 <= grid[i][j] <= 50`",
  "constraints": [
    "n == grid.length == grid[i].length",
    "1 <= n <= 50",
    "0 <= grid[i][j] <= 50"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[1,2],[3,4]]",
      "output": "17",
      "explanation": "Here are the three projections (\"shadows\") of the shape made with each axis-aligned plane."
    },
